Title: PHP, libapreq-1 and CVE-2026-109618
ACL: joe=rw, *=r
status: draft

Rasmus Lerdorf personally [ported libapreq-1](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/master/main/rfc1867.c) to the PHP runtime decades ago. **That port remains secure**. **CVE-2026-109618** is a novel, sophisticated attack on the PHP runtime itself (misuse of `longjmp()` under OOM), exacerbated by the browser industry's unwillingness to meaningfully secure the file upload process by otherwise providing human users with file upload performance metadata widgetry in the browser.

Cynically, but honestly, this is simply because they don't want your bug reports about non-performing uploads. Sadly, abrogation of responsiblity informs most of the division of labor (yours, or mine) in protocol standards development.

This missing functionality puts the burden of responsibility where it never belonged: on server-side API hooks into the in-flight upload apparatus itself.

@sunstarsys/status/2113323849794380204

NOTE: An important mitigator for Linux for this vulnerability is to set `memlimit=-1` in your php configuration, and on the host run

  ```shell
    sysctl vm.overcommit_memory=1
  ```
as root, so your PHP webserver process gets OOMKilled by the kernel instead of receiving soft allocation errors on `realloc(3)`, which woould otherwise trigger the problematic `longjmp(3)`...


